		Block:
			Id: front
			Text: Urban Ecology Notes · Research article
				Style: journal
			Heading 1:
				Id: title
				Style: paper-title
				In table of contents: false
				Text: Occupancy of integrated nest bricks by common swifts in retrofitted housing
			Paragraph: Maren Holt[1]{sup}, Tomás Ferreira[2]{sup} and Aiyana Brooks[1]{sup}
				Style: authors
			Paragraph: [1]{sup} Department of Ecology, Northfield University · [2]{sup} Northfield City Housing Trust · Correspondence: [m.holt@northfield-uni.example](mailto:m.holt@northfield-uni.example)
				Style: affiliations
			Block:
				Style: abstract
				Text: Abstract
					Style: abstract-label
				Paragraph: Energy retrofits seal the gaps under roofs where common swifts (*Apus apus*) nest. We followed 96 nest bricks built into the new insulation of twelve apartment blocks, and 48 boxes mounted on the surface of the same façades, for four breeding seasons. Occupancy of the integrated bricks rose from 18% in the first season to 66% in the fourth, and stayed below 40% in the surface boxes. Bricks on north and east façades, and those more than 9 m above the ground, were occupied earlier. Playing swift calls during the first season doubled early occupancy. We conclude that integrated bricks are an effective, low-cost measure when they are planned with the insulation and placed high on the cooler façades.
				Paragraph: **Keywords:** Apus apus; nest bricks; building retrofit; urban birds; monitoring
					Style: keywords
		Columns:
			Id: body
			Style: body
			# Each section is a Block with its heading and its parts, and each part (a subsection, or the paragraphs and
			# figures between two figures) is another Block. They have no box of their own: they group what the template
			# repeats.
			Block:
				Heading 1:
					Id: introduction
					Text: Introduction
				Block:
					Paragraph: The common swift breeds almost only in buildings in much of Europe. It nests in cavities under roof tiles, behind fascia boards and in holes in old masonry, and it returns to the same cavity every year [[{number of: ref-mayer}](#ref-mayer)]. When a building is insulated from the outside, those cavities are covered, often while the birds are away in Africa, and a colony can lose all its sites in a single winter [[{number of: ref-ostrowski}](#ref-ostrowski)].
					Paragraph: Two measures are common. A *nest brick* is a hollow block built into the wall or the insulation, flush with the façade, with an entrance slot on its lower face. A *surface box* is fixed to the finished wall. Bricks are cheaper to maintain and do not affect the appearance of the building, but they must be planned before the works begin[^planning].
					Paragraph: This study compares both measures on the same buildings over four breeding seasons and asks which placement leads to faster occupancy.
			Block:
				Heading 1:
					Id: methods
					Text: Methods
				Block:
					Heading 2:
						Id: sites
						Text: Study sites
					Paragraph: The twelve blocks belong to the Northfield City Housing Trust and were insulated between October 2021 and March 2022. All of them had held swift nests before the works. The sites are described in Table {number of: tab-sites}, and the position of the bricks on a typical façade is shown in Figure {number of: fig-facade}.
					Figure:
						Id: tab-sites
						Caption: The twelve blocks grouped by district, with the bricks and boxes installed.
