					Figure:
						Id: fig-facade
						Caption: A typical façade: four bricks below the eaves, two boxes on the gable.
						Image: documents/paper/facade.svg
							Alt: Drawing of a four-storey façade with four nest bricks just below the eaves and two boxes mounted on the gable end
							Box:
								Width: 100%
				Block:
					Heading 2:
						Id: monitoring
						Text: Monitoring
					Paragraph: Every brick and box was checked from the ground at dusk on six evenings between 20 May and 25 July of each year, from 2022 to 2025. A site was counted as occupied when an adult entered it at least twice on separate evenings[^occupied]. On half of the blocks, chosen at random, swift calls were played from a speaker on the roof for one hour before dusk during May and June of 2022.
					Paragraph: The effect of season, measure, façade orientation and height on occupancy was estimated with a mixed logistic model, with the block as a random effect.
			Block:
				Heading 1:
					Id: results
					Text: Results
				Block:
					Paragraph: Occupancy grew in each season for both measures, but faster for the bricks (Figure {number of: fig-occupancy}). By 2025, 63 of the 96 bricks (66%) and 18 of the 48 boxes (38%) were occupied. The difference was already clear in the second season (odds ratio 2.1, 95% CI 1.3–3.4).
					Figure:
						Id: fig-occupancy
						Caption: Share of nest sites occupied in each breeding season.
						Image: documents/paper/occupancy.svg
							Alt: Bar chart of the occupancy of nest sites per season. Integrated bricks: 18% in 2022, 41% in 2023, 57% in 2024 and 66% in 2025. Surface boxes: 12%, 27%, 35% and 38%.
							Box:
								Width: 100%
				Block:
					Paragraph: Bricks on north and east façades were occupied one season earlier than those facing south or west, where the temperature inside a test brick reached 41 °C on sunny afternoons. Height mattered as well: no brick below 9 m was occupied before the third season.
					Paragraph: On the blocks where calls were played, occupancy in 2022 was 26%, against 11% on the others. The difference narrowed in later years and was no longer significant in 2025.
			Block:
				Heading 1:
					Id: discussion
					Text: Discussion
				Block:
					Paragraph: Integrated bricks were taken up faster than surface boxes on the same buildings (Figure {number of: fig-occupancy}, page {page of: fig-occupancy}), which agrees with earlier, shorter studies [[{number of: ref-ostrowski}](#ref-ostrowski), [{number of: ref-lindgren}](#ref-lindgren)]. A likely reason is the climate inside the cavity: the brick sits within the insulation, while the box takes the full heat of the wall[^temperature].
					Paragraph: Calls attract prospecting birds, which then return to the site in the following year. They are most useful in the first season, when the colony has just lost its old cavities and is looking for new ones.
					Paragraph: Our blocks were similar to each other, and the results may not hold for brick terraces or for buildings without a former colony. The cost figures, discussed in the trust’s report [[{number of: ref-nchc}](#ref-nchc)], are those of 2022.
			Block:
				Heading 1:
					Id: conclusions
					Text: Conclusions
				Block:
					List:
						Style: findings
						List item:
							Paragraph: Plan nest bricks with the insulation, not after it.
						List item:
							Paragraph: Place them on the north and east façades, more than 9 m above the ground.
						List item:
							Paragraph: Play swift calls during the first season after the works.
			Heading 1:
				Id: acknowledgements
				Style: unnumbered
				Text: Acknowledgements
			Paragraph: We thank the tenants of the twelve blocks for their patience, and the volunteers of the Northfield Swift Group for 2,300 hours of evening watches.
			Heading 1:
				Id: references
				Style: unnumbered
				Text: References
			List:
				Style: references
				List item:
					Id: ref-mayer
					Paragraph: Mayer, L. and Sato, K. (2019). Site fidelity of common swifts in central European towns. *Journal of Avian Ecology* 41: 112–125.
				List item:
					Id: ref-ostrowski
					Paragraph: Ostrowski, P. (2021). Loss of swift colonies after façade insulation in Poland. *Urban Birds* 8: 33–47.
				List item:
					Id: ref-lindgren
					Paragraph: Lindgren, E., Holt, M. and Varga, B. (2023). Nest boxes and bricks for swifts: a two-year trial. *Bird Conservation Notes* 15: 201–210.
				List item:
					Id: ref-nchc
					Paragraph: Northfield City Housing Trust (2022). *Retrofit programme 2021–2022: final report*. Northfield.
			Footnote:
				Id: planning
				Paragraph: A brick added after the works means cutting the new insulation and its render again, which costs about four times as much.
			Footnote:
				Id: occupied
				Paragraph: One entry may be a prospecting bird. Two entries on separate evenings are the usual criterion for a breeding pair.
			Footnote:
				Id: temperature
				Paragraph: Loggers in two bricks and two boxes on a south façade recorded a mean afternoon maximum of 31 °C in the bricks and 37 °C in the boxes in July 2024.
